# pacman — real-world examples

Arch Linux package manager (on the Tumbleweed homelab use `zypper` instead).
All commands accept multiple package names.

## Search, install, remove

```sh
pacman -Ss <package name>    # search
pacman -S <package name>     # install
pacman -R <package name>     # uninstall
pacman -Rcs <package name>   # uninstall + dependencies, removing new orphans
```

## Update the system

```sh
pacman -Syu                  # refresh package database and upgrade everything
```

## Query packages

```sh
pacman -Si <package name>    # package information
pacman -Qe                   # explicitly installed packages
pacman -Qdt                  # orphans (installed as deps, no longer required)

# Commands provided by an installed package
pacman -Ql <package name> | sed -n -e 's/.*\/bin\///p' | tail -n +2
```

## Install from a built package file

```sh
pacman -U <file name/file url>
```

## Build and install from the AUR

```sh
# pacman can't install from the AUR directly. Modern helpers: yay, paru.
# Manual build with makepkg (assuming $pkgname holds the package name):
wget "https://aur.archlinux.org/packages/${pkgname::2}/$pkgname/$pkgname.tar.gz"
tar zxvf "$pkgname.tar.gz"
cd "$pkgname"
makepkg -s                   # -s installs build dependencies automatically
sudo pacman -U <package file (.pkg.tar.xz)>
```

| Flag | Meaning                                  |
| ---- | ---------------------------------------- |
| -S   | install from repos                       |
| -Ss  | search repos                             |
| -Syu | sync database + full upgrade             |
| -R   | remove                                   |
| -Rcs | remove with deps and orphans             |
| -Si  | repo package info                        |
| -Qe  | list explicitly installed                |
| -Qdt | list orphans                             |
| -Ql  | list files owned by installed package    |
| -U   | install from local/remote package file   |
